```yaml
verdict: approve-with-comments
concerns:
  - "AC2 pins live counts against this repo's current .speccraft/ files (55 heading lines for conventions.md, 6 for architecture.md). The next time architecture.md grows a 7th top-level heading — a natural side-effect of any close that lands an ADR — the test fails for the correct reason (a heading was added) and someone bumps the number instead of asking whether the guarantee still holds. The test should ride a curated fixture that pins the SHAPE of the emission and use the live files only for the byte-savings ratio in AC4, per the fixture-first meta-guard regime in conventions.md."
  - "AC3 asserts absence of reference-file body text by grepping for 'a string that appears in its body but in no heading' of THIS repo's conventions.md. That is an unnamed live-content dependency (which string? enforced by what?) and slides straight into the spec-0049 'prove every negative assertion bites' failure mode — a body string that later moves into a heading, or a rewording, makes the negative assertion vacuously pass. Curate a fixture whose body contains a deterministic sentinel token that cannot appear in any heading by construction (e.g. a lowercase multi-word phrase the heading grammar excludes), and assert its absence."
  - "AC12 states what templates/prompts/review.md must convey in prose but gives no mechanically checkable predicate. This is exactly the shape spec 0014's 'structural over content' rule was written for — either pin specific literal marker strings the template MUST contain (and grep for them from tests/hooks/spec-review-payload.bats), or drop AC12 to a design note and let AC3+AC6+AC11 carry the enforceable weight."
  - "AC7's 'every selected agent refused → stop without invoking cross-reviewer or writing review.md, leaving any prior review.md and its reviewed_sha256 byte-unchanged' is load-bearing for the --diff transaction's provenance gate (spec 0035): if a refusal-only round wiped or rewrote review.md, the next --diff would classify as full-review and the whole savings would collapse. AC7 says the bytes are unchanged, but does not require the assertion pattern to also check the FILE's mtime/inode is unchanged OR — better — that no atomicRename call fired. Pinning it via the spec-0035 atomicRename seam (assert zero rename attempts against .../review.md on all-refused) would make this deterministic rather than 'we didn't write it, honest'."
  - "The interaction with the --diff scoped-payload builder (spec 0035's review_build_payload) is only addressed by AC9 for the spec-content source. The composed payload for --diff still needs to obey the byte budget — presumably it does via review_compose_payload — but AC9 doesn't pin that path against the budget. A scoped --diff review whose delta is small but whose reference-tier heading index is huge could still refuse under argv mode. State explicitly that the budget check runs on the composed payload from BOTH review_build_payload (full/short-circuit) and the scoped path."
  - "REVIEW_MAX_ARGV_BYTES = 65536 is derived from the field report's '~70 KB working limit', rounded down. That derivation is worth pinning in a comment beside the constant, not just in the spec's Why — a later contributor with no memory of the field incident will 'obviously' bump it to 131072 the first time a big spec refuses, and the timeout returns as a mystery."
suggestions:
  - "Move AC2's per-file expected shape onto a checked-in fixture under tests/hooks/fixtures/spec-review-payload/{conventions,architecture}.md whose content is chosen to exercise: no headings; only ## headings; only ### headings; a heading containing ``code``; a heading with unicode; a body line that starts with `## ` inside a fenced code block (must NOT be extracted as a heading — cf. conventions.md §fixture-first-per-tool meta-guard, and the classic bug where a shell fixture literally uses `## Header` in a heredoc). Keep AC4's ratio check against live .speccraft/ files, since that's what asserts the actual saving."
  - "Add an AC for CODE-BLOCK-BLINDNESS in review_heading_index. Conventions.md's own body carries ```md fenced examples that contain `## ` and `### ` inside them; a naive `grep -E '^##[# ]?'` would emit false headings. Pin it with a fixture whose fenced block contains `## Not a real heading` and assert it does NOT appear in the index."
  - "Consider whether refusal should be blocking-at-command-level or blocking-per-agent when the developer explicitly named --agent claude-p (i.e. picked a specific agent for this round). The spec's current 'refused agents don't count toward quorum' is right for a quorum-of-N invocation, but for an explicit single-agent round it degrades silently to 'the agent you asked for was refused → 0 verdicts → below quorum → command stops'. That IS a legible failure per AC7, so this is a nit — but an explicit note in AC7 that a single-agent-picked round exits non-zero (rather than 0) when its sole agent is refused would prevent a scripting surprise."
  - "AC10 makes REVIEW_MAX_ARGV_BYTES/REVIEW_MAX_PAYLOAD_BYTES env-overridable following HISTORY_WINDOW_N precedent — good. Add that an env override BELOW 1024 (or 0, or negative, or non-numeric) is rejected with a named error and the default is used, so a `REVIEW_MAX_ARGV_BYTES=0` in a shell rc doesn't silently refuse every review with a cryptic 'argv-limit exceeded: 100 > 0'."
  - "The advisory for existing repos (§What #3) is worth a follow-up spec entry now (as an open question or explicit deferred: line): what does /speccraft:sync do about a .speccraft/agents.toml that still has input='argv'? The most obvious answer — surface it in the drift report — is already how spec 0034's conventions.md marker drift is handled and would give repos a durable nudge to migrate rather than only seeing the message when a review actually refuses."
  - "AC5's `refuse:<reason>` shape is good, but pin the enum of reasons exhaustively (argv-limit, payload-limit; and reject anything else at test time) so a future 'refuse:something-else' does not leak silently past AC6's message assertions."
guardrail_violations: []
convention_violations:
  - rule: "Structural over content (conventions.md §E2E assertion predicates)"
    location: "AC2 (line-count pins), AC3 (unnamed body-string dependency), AC12 (prose-only predicate)"
  - rule: "Prove every negative assertion bites (conventions.md §Prove every negative assertion bites)"
    location: "AC3 — the grep-for-body-string-absent check is exactly the shape that goes inert when the string moves; the mitigation is a curated sentinel token in a fixture, not the live file"
  - rule: "Fixture-first per-tool meta-guard matching regime (conventions.md §Fixture-first, per-tool meta-guard matching regime)"
    location: "AC2 and AC3 — no forbidden/permitted fixtures are named; the check operates only against live repo files"
```

## Discussion

The spec is strong overall. It identifies the failure mode precisely (622 KB payload, argv vs stdin asymmetry, timeout as least-informative outcome), quantifies the saving (~1/40th via heading index), and — crucially — refuses the two seductively bad alternatives explicitly in §Out of scope: no auto-fallback from argv to stdin (would silence the failure), no section-scoped excerpting (heading-heuristic misses = silent guardrail skip). Both rejections align with the durable "fail loud, refuse legibly" pattern that runs through specs 0035, 0048, 0049, 0050. The interaction with the spec-0035 --diff single-read transaction is thought about (AC9) rather than left as a landmine.

The layered design — tiered context assembly + pre-dispatch budget + template default flip — is the right shape: the tier split does the real work, the budget check makes remaining oversize legible, and the template change opts new repos out by default while advising existing repos. The two-tier split (INLINE vs REFERENCE) is more defensible than a heuristic threshold; tier is a property of the file, fixed in the lib. Good.

The main risks are all in the test authoring, not the design. AC2/AC3/AC12 each drift toward the "assertion measures live repo state" trap that this codebase has been bitten by before (spec 0033's macOS symlink issue with normalization on both sides; spec 0049's inert `! grep -q`; spec 0050's macOS grep `--version` mis-parse). The fix is small: move the mechanical shape assertions onto a fixture, keep the byte-savings ratio check against live files. That preserves AC4's value (the saving exists in reality, not just in a fixture) without pinning AC2 to counts that a future close will change.

One deliberate call I'd flag rather than object to: the spec confines itself to `/speccraft:spec:review` and explicitly defers `/speccraft:spec:review-code`, `/speccraft:arch:review`, `/speccraft:pm:review` to a follow-up "once the helpers have proven out." That's the right call given review-code shares the pattern (per the spec's own note) but the surface area is different enough that generalizing prematurely would over-fit. Just make sure the follow-up is filed at close, not left as tribal knowledge.

The bump-and-close sequencing implication: the template change to `input = "stdin"` and the new `review.lib.sh` helpers are user-facing behavior changes to `commands/spec/review.md`, so this will carry a MINOR bump on release. Per the bump-before-close rule in memory ([version-bump before spec:close](feedback_bump_before_close.md)), do the six-location version bump INSIDE this spec BEFORE spec:close, or roll into a release spec with any others queued. Just a heads-up, not a spec issue.
